{"format_version":"vbulletin-3+-thread","url":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/main-forum/the-classic-world-of-darkness/930162-automatic-damage-and-other-combat-house-rules-i-use","id":930162,"title":"Automatic damage and other combat house rules I use.","path":["Home","Onyx Path","White Wolf Worlds","World of Darkness"],"posts":[{"date":"2016-07-11T21:27:42-07:00","id":930162,"title":"Automatic damage and other combat house rules I use.","content":"Some of these may already exist, but my eyes tend to glaze over when I read the Drama section of WoD books and I might miss stuff.<br /> <br /> <br /> * Normal RPG-style initiative. I don't use WoD-style backwards initiative because it's unintuitive and slows things down. I do use 1E style damage modifiers (-0,-1,-2,-3,-4,-5, Inc, etc.) so there's still plenty of advantage to not going last.<br /> <br /> * Automatic damage (but not like NWoD). Attacks do attack successes + weapon damage in health levels against the target. Light pistols are +0 health levels, heavy pistols are +1 health levels. Damage for melee and unarmed combat is an average a value determined by strength (-1 to +3 for strength 1-5 respectively) and the weapon damage, rounded up.<br /> <br /> * Automatic soak. Half the regular soak in automatic reduction of health level damage. When the regular soak would be odd, round up the first time you get hit in an action scene, and alternate rounding down and up for the rest of the scene.<br /> <br /> *Defensive actions not on your turn. In my experience people usually don't save Dodge dice in a held action. They also get upset when they can't do a defensive action because their opponent acts before them. I think both these positions are reasonable. I add a +1 difficulty for off-turn defensive actions (same as if you'd changed your action in the backwards initiative system). A defensive action can be a dodge, a block, a block and counterattack, a dodge that brings you into cover (as most dodges do, ideally), countermagic, closing a reinforced door, maneuvering a vehicle in such a way that multiple people are protected from attack, etc. If you still have a turn this round, you lose it. If you don't, you lose next round's turn. Hopefully you get yourself into a better position so you're not always on the defensive.<br /> <br /> * Multiple shots. If it's the same target, each extra shot simply gives one extra die to the attack. The returns diminish considerably for fully automatic weapons. When attacking multiple targets, split the dice pool as normal and do not add dice unless more than one round goes to the same target. <i>Only if it's a heavy pistol</i>, add a non-cumulative +1 difficulty for all targets after the first.<br /> <br /> * Stickup maneuver. If you get the drop on someone and have a weapon leveled at them before a combat scene starts (ie before initiative is rolled), and they decide to attack you anyway, you're at +5 to initiative for the subsequent combat scene and if they hesitate at all you are considered to have &quot;taken aim&quot; for a round (+1 die to the first attack).<br /> <br /> * Indiana Jones Maneuver. If you have a firearm and someone rushes you from more than 1/3 their movement distance away, you get an extra &quot;attack of opportunity&quot; against them at +2 difficulty. This counts against your weapon's firing rate for the current turn, or the next turn if you're used up your weapon's maximum for this turn.<br /> <br /> * Revolvers treat most botches as simple failures. They are more reliable than clip-fed guns, less likely to jam.","signature":"","edited":{"userid":1503,"user_link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/1503-faradn","user":"Faradn","date":". 07-12-2016, 01:09 AM"},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/1503-faradn","name":"Faradn","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar1503_2.jpeg","title":"Member","rank":0,"rank-name":"","custom":